-      TASK
-      Implement and evaluate a default-off production Safety-Net adapter. Disabled behavior must be byte-identical to native VerifyRepairPipeline behavior. Enabled routing may reorder or abstain but must preserve all candidates and native exact fallback.
-
-      CONCRETE STEPS
-      0. PRECONDITIONS: evaluate the structured gate; record expected and observed values, upstream artifact path and hash, Python and Z3 versions, CPU/RAM/disk, fixture and router hashes, and protected-file hashes.
-      1. Ensure the relevant production-adapter capability spec has REQ-* and SCENARIO anchors for default-off identity, typed configuration, candidate preservation, abstention, exception immutability, exact fallback, rollback, and errors. Write tests first.
-      2. Add the smallest production module under python/carnot/pipeline/. Reuse the frozen V566 feature and router contract. Do not copy held rows, outcomes, or entity identifiers into policy state.
-      3. Make the adapter disabled by default. Prove byte-identical serialized requests, candidate order, checker calls, outputs, error types, side effects, and persistence behavior on a frozen regression bank.
-      4. In the enabled arm, preserve every candidate. Record route, abstention, exception lookup, fallback reason, exact result, and charged adapter overhead. Any unsupported or uncertain input must fall back.
-      5. Compare native, disabled-adapter, enabled-router, forced-abstain, forced-fallback, rollback, and malformed-input conditions on identical units and seeds.
-      6. Attack model and family IDs, source IDs, entity names, row order, held table writes, candidate deletion, fallback recursion, serialization changes, stale configuration, and disabled-path side effects.
-      7. Set production_safety_net_adapter_ready_score=1.0 only when disabled identity is exact, enabled decisions match the frozen router, all exact outputs remain equal, fallback is reachable, and every attack fails closed.
-      8. Write one terminal artifact atomically. Run focused tests with full new-module coverage, lint, spec coverage, row lint, gate audit, adversarial verification, pipeline E2E checks, and git status.
